Oratcl

INSTALLATION

Oratcl uses the standard Tcl Extension Architecture (TEA) build system
(GNU Autoconf + make), the same workflow on macOS, Linux/Unix, and
Windows (via cross-compilation -- see below). There is no separate
per-platform build system or directory; the same configure.in/Makefile.in
is used everywhere.

Requirements:

  1. A working Tcl 9 installation (headers + tclConfig.sh)
  2. A C compiler (gcc/clang; MSVC is not required, see "Windows" below)

Notably, NO Oracle client software, headers, or libraries are required
to build Oratcl, on any platform. Oratcl dynamically loads the Oracle
client library (dlopen/dlsym on Unix/macOS, LoadLibrary/GetProcAddress
on Windows) at runtime, not link time -- so you can build Oratcl on a
machine with no Oracle software installed at all, and deploy the
resulting binary to a different machine that has a real Oracle client.


BUILDING (macOS / Linux / Unix, native)

Change to the toplevel directory and run:

  ./configure --with-tcl=DIR --with-oracle-directory=DIR
  make
  make install

  --with-tcl=DIR              directory containing tclConfig.sh
                               (e.g. /opt/homebrew/lib on an Apple
                               Silicon Homebrew install, or
                               $ORACLE_HOME/../tcl/lib, etc.)

  --with-oracle-directory=DIR directory containing an sdk/include
                               subfolder. This only needs to satisfy a
                               configure check for a *path*; since no
                               Oracle header is actually #included by
                               Oratcl's own source, even an empty
                               placeholder directory with a sdk/include
                               subfolder works fine if you don't have
                               a real Oracle Instant Client SDK handy.

  --prefix=DIR                where to install (default: same prefix
                               as your Tcl installation)

  --disable-shared             build a static library instead of the
                               default shared library

"make install" copies the resulting shared library, library/*.tcl
support files, and a generated pkgIndex.tcl into a versioned Oratcl
directory under your Tcl installation's package path.

At runtime, Oratcl needs to find the real Oracle client shared library
(libclntsh.so/.dylib). By default it looks for
$ORACLE_HOME/lib/libclntsh<ext>; if your Oracle Instant Client doesn't
follow that layout (a plain Instant Client download places
libclntsh.dylib/.so directly in its root, not under a lib/
subdirectory), set env(ORACLE_LIBRARY) to the full path of the library
file before loading the package.


BUILDING (Windows, via cross-compilation)

The recommended way to build Oratcl for Windows is to cross-compile it
from Linux (including WSL Ubuntu running on Windows itself) using the
free mingw-w64 toolchain -- no Visual Studio or MSVC license is
required. See doc/Oratcl_Users_Guide_and_Reference.md's "MS Windows"
section for the full, verified recipe. In short:

  sudo apt install mingw-w64 build-essential

  # Cross-build Tcl 9 itself for Windows first, to guarantee ABI
  # compatibility with the Oratcl build:
  cd tcl9.x.y/win
  ./configure --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 --enable-64bit --prefix=DIR
  make && make install

  # Then cross-configure and build Oratcl the same way as above, adding
  # --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32:
  ./configure --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 --with-tcl=DIR/lib \
      --with-oracle-directory=DIR
  make

This produces a tcl9Oratcl<version>.dll, which can be copied alongside
pkgIndex.tcl and library/oratcl_utils.tcl to a target Windows machine
with a native Windows Tcl 9 installation. As on other platforms, no
Oracle software is required to build it, but you do need the Oracle
Instant Client for Windows present (with its directory added to
%PATH%) to actually connect to a database at runtime.


TESTING THE BUILD

"make test" runs the test suite (requires a live Oracle database;
see tests/defs.tcl for the environment variables used to configure the
test connection).

You can also try a quick interactive smoke test:

  $ tclsh9.0
  % package require Oratcl
  % oralogon scott/tiger       ** use a valid connect string **
  % oraopen oratcl0
  % orasql oratcl0.0 {select * from emp}
  % orafetch oratcl0.0
  % oracols oratcl0.0
  % oramsg oratcl0.0 all
